<task>
    Extract the Apple device configuration described in a classified-ad HTML
    snippet, and return it as a single JSON object. Follow the &lt;procedure&gt;
    exactly — it is a fixed checklist, not optional.
</task>

<input>
HTML_CONTENT:
{{adHtmlContent}}

Read only the ad's title, main description text, and any structured
spec/attribute fields. Ignore CSS, &lt;script&gt; blocks, nav/menu chrome,
and images.

STOP READING at the first occurrence of any of these section markers
(and anything after them): "Das könnte dich auch interessieren",
"Weitere Anzeigen für dich", "Mehr von diesem Anbieter", or close
variants. Do this filtering FIRST, before extraction.
</input>

<reference_data>
    See attached device catalog JSON. Each device row has an `id` and allowed
    value lists: `silicon[]`, `colors[]`, `storage[]`, `unified_memory[]`,
    each with their own `id`.
</reference_data>

<procedure>
    Work through these steps in order. Keep work internal; output only the
    final JSON.

    STEP 1 — Locate the device family.
    Scan for an explicit Model Number (format A####, e.g. "A2337"). If found,
    that uniquely fixes `model_id` — skip to STEP 3 for that row only.
    If absent, narrow by product line + size + generation hints (e.g.
    "MacBook Pro 14 Zoll", "iPad Air", release year, "Touch Bar" vs no
    Touch Bar). Shortlist every catalog row that's still plausible.

    STEP 2 — Pick the primary row.
    From the shortlist, choose the single most likely row using context
    (typical default configs, release recency, popularity). Put it in
    `model_id`. Put the rest of the shortlist into `alternative_candidates`,
    most-plausible first. If the shortlist had only one row, leave
    `alternative_candidates` as [].

    STEP 3 — Extract raw attribute text.
    Pull out, verbatim from the ad, anything resembling: chip name/generation
    (M1–M6 or later, A-series, plus Pro/Max/Ultra variant), CPU core count, GPU core
    count, color name, storage size, unified memory size. Don't map to IDs
    yet — just capture the text.
    Core counts: record CPU and GPU core counts SEPARATELY, and only
    assign a number to CPU or GPU if a keyword is attached to it:
    - GPU: "GPU", "Grafik", "Grafikkerne", "GPU-Kerne"
    (e.g. "10-Core GPU", "10 Grafikkerne", "GPU 10C" → GPU = 10).
    - CPU: "CPU", "Prozessor", "Prozessorkerne", "CPU-Kerne"
    (e.g. "8-Core CPU", "8C CPU" → CPU = 8).
    - Combined shorthand "8C/10C", "8-Core CPU / 10-Core GPU", "8/10
    Kerne" → first = CPU, second = GPU.
    - A core count with no CPU/GPU keyword ("10-Core", "10 Kerne") →
    keep it as unassigned; do not guess which one it is.
    If a core type is not mentioned, record it as "not stated" — never
    fill it in from the chip's base or default configuration.

    STEP 4 — Normalize and map to IDs (within the chosen row only).
    Before comparing chip text to `silicon[]` entries, normalize both sides:
    lowercase, treat "-", "‑" (U+2011), and "–" as equivalent, and treat
    "mit"/"und" as equivalent to "with"/"and"/"+". A chip match requires:
    same chip generation (M4 ≠ M5), same Pro/Max/Ultra tier, AND matching
    CPU-core and GPU-core numbers.

    STEP 4-chip — Choosing silicon_id when core counts are partial.
    Every core count the ad DOES state is a hard filter; a core count the
    ad does NOT state is a wildcard. Apply in this order:
    0. Take the chosen row's silicon[] ids and look up each label in the
    top-level `silicon` table. Read the CPU and GPU core counts from the
    label ("M3 mit 8‑Core CPU und 10‑Core GPU" → CPU 8, GPU 10).
    1. Keep only silicon[] entries with the right generation and tier.
    2. If the ad states a GPU core count, remove every entry whose GPU
    core count differs. If it states a CPU core count, do the same for
    CPU. Example: row 8 has silicon [16, 17]. The ad says "M3 … 10-Core
    GPU" with no CPU count → 16 (8-core GPU) is removed, 17 (10-core GPU)
    stays → silicon_id = 17. Do NOT pick 16 just because it is the base
    model or comes first in the list.
    3. An unassigned core count from STEP 3 (no CPU/GPU keyword) is kept
    only if it matches exactly one of CPU or GPU across the remaining
    entries; then use it as a filter. Otherwise ignore it.
    4. Exactly one entry left → use its id. More than one left → pick
    the one with the lowest unstated core count (the base config that
    still satisfies everything stated), and lower STEP 6 confidence.
    Zero left → re-check STEP 2 (another row may fit); if still none,
    set silicon_id to null. Never fall back to an entry that contradicts
    a stated core count.
    The base or most popular chip config is ONLY a tie-breaker in step 4.
    It never overrides a core count written in the ad.
    For each of silicon_id / color_id / storage_id / memory_id:
    - Special rule: If the model only has one possible silicon in the
    `silicon[]` list in the reference data always use that silicon. Same
    goes for memory. If silicon[] has several entries, use STEP 4-chip.
    - If the normalized ad text matches an entry in that row's allowed
    list → use that entry's id.
    - If the ad gives a value but it does NOT appear in the row's allowed
    list → this is a signal STEP 2 may have picked the wrong row.
    Re-check STEP 2 before finalizing; if still no match, set to null.
    - If the ad simply never mentions that attribute → null.
    Never output an id that isn't in the matched row's own list (hard
    constraint — this overrides any other instinct).

    STEP 4a — Storage vs. memory disambiguation (German ads).
    Ads are normally in German. Storage and memory are both expressed as
    plain &lt;number&gt; values, so do not rely on magnitude or position
    alone — anchor on keywords first:
    - STORAGE keywords: "SSD", "Festplatte", "interner Speicher",
    "Speicherplatz", "Speicherkapazität". Storage may be given in GB
    (256GB, 512GB) or TB (1TB, 2TB, 4TB, 8TB, 16TB) — TB is ALWAYS storage,
    never memory.
    - MEMORY keywords: "RAM", "Arbeitsspeicher", "Hauptspeicher",
    "Unified Memory", "Memory". Memory is always given in GB, never TB.
    - Combined shorthand is common, e.g. "8/256GB", "16/512GB", or
    "256GB / 16GB RAM". If a keyword is attached to at least one of
    the two numbers, use it to anchor that one, then assign the
    remaining number to the other attribute. Only fall back to the
    conventional "RAM/Storage" ordering (first number = memory,
    second = storage) when NEITHER number has a keyword to anchor on
    — and reflect that extra uncertainty in STEP 6's confidence score.
    - Some sizes exist in BOTH the storage and memory allowed lists for
    a given row (e.g. 64GB and 128GB can be either, depending on the
    Pro/Max configuration). A bare number with no keyword and no
    paired second number is NOT safe to assign — leave it null rather
    than guessing which field it belongs to.
    - Normalize formatting before matching: "GB"/"Gb"/"gb"/"Giga"/
    "Gigabyte" are equivalent, and ignore spaces between number and
    unit ("512 GB" = "512GB"). Treat "1TB"/"2TB"/etc. as the matching
    catalog TB entry directly — don't convert it to a GB figure.

    STEP 4b — Color mapping.
    Catalog color labels are German. Ads often use English or mixed
    spellings. Normalize (lowercase, ignore spaces/hyphens, "grey" =
    "gray"), then map to the German label and look up its id:
    - "space grey", "space gray", "spacegrau", "space grau", "grau" → "Space Grau"
    - "silver", "silber" → "Silber"
    - "gold" → "Gold"
    - "midnight", "mitternacht", "mitternachtsblau" → "Mitternacht"
    - "starlight", "polarstern" → "Polarstern"
    - "space black", "space schwarz", "schwarz" → "Space Schwarz"
    - "sky blue", "himmelblau", "hellblau" → "Himmelblau"
    - "pink", "rose", "rosa" → "Rosa"
    - "citrus", "zitrus", "gelb" → "Zitrus"
    - "indigo" → "Indigo"
    The resulting id must be in the chosen row's colors[] list; otherwise
    follow the "not in the row's list" rule in STEP 4. A generic word
    like "schwarz" or "grau" maps only if exactly one of the row's
    colors fits; if not, set color_id to null.
    Example: row 8 has colors [1, 2, 4, 5]; "space grey" → "Space Grau"
    = id 2 → color_id = 2.

    STEP 4c — Device condition fields (serial number, battery).
    These are free-value fields, not catalog IDs. Extract them only when
    the ad states them explicitly; otherwise set the field to the JSON
    literal null (unquoted). Never infer, estimate, or derive them from
    other attributes, and never output a placeholder like "N/A",
    "unknown", -1, 0, or "" — use null. The "N/A" rule from STEP 7 does
    NOT apply here.
    - serial_number: keywords "Seriennummer", "Serial", "S/N", "SN".
    Copy the value verbatim (uppercase, spaces removed). If the seller
    says it is available on request ("auf Anfrage", "per PN") or the
    value is masked/partial (e.g. "C02X****"), set null.
    - battery_cycle_count: keywords "Ladezyklen", "Zyklen",
    "Akkuzyklen", "Cycle Count". Output an integer (e.g. "nur 87
    Ladezyklen" → 87). Vague text ("wenige Zyklen", "kaum benutzt")
    → null.
    - battery_max_capacity: keywords "Akkukapazität", "Maximale
    Kapazität", "Akkuzustand", "Batteriezustand", "Battery Health",
    "Kapazität". Output an integer percentage without the "%" sign
    (e.g. "Akku 92%" → 92). Values outside 0–100, mAh/Wh figures, or
    vague text ("Akku top", "hält lange") → null.
    - Do not confuse these numbers with storage/memory values from
    STEP 4a, and do not let them influence model or attribute matching.
    These fields do not affect the STEP 6 confidence score.

    STEP 5 — iPad cellular rule.
    If the matched row is an iPad and the ad text contains no explicit
    cellular indicator ("LTE", "5G", "Cellular", "SIM"), prefer the Wi‑Fi-only
    sibling row over the Wi‑Fi+Cellular row.

    STEP 6 — Confidence.
    - 8–10: Model Number was explicit (STEP 1 shortcut), OR every extracted
    attribute mapped unambiguously with zero competing rows.
    - 4–7: Device class is clear but model_id required inference (STEP 2),
    OR alternative_candidates is non-empty, OR one or more attribute ids
    are null due to genuine ambiguity/silence in the ad.
    - 1–3: Ad text is sparse, contradictory, or chaotic enough that the
    device identity itself is in real doubt.

    STEP 7 — Seller identity.
    Look in the ad body, profile snippet, or metadata for a name/username.
    "Privatanbieter: Max Mustermann" → first="Max", last="Mustermann".
    A single name/handle → first_name only, last_name="N/A". Always capitalize
    the first and last name. If either the first or last name cannot be determined,
    set the missing field to "N/A". If no name or username is found, set both
    first_name and last_name to "N/A".
</procedure>